 1999.</p>\\n    </div>\\n  </div>\\n</footer>\\n\\n</body>\\n</html>\",\"improvements\":[{\"What\":\"Added a featured product grid with Add to Cart buttons\",\"Why\":\"Lets shoppers buy from the homepage, raising conversions\",\"Hash\":\"HDLmClass26dbdf39\"},{\"What\":\"Added a Shop All Products link\",\"Why\":\"Guides visitors into the full catalog\",\"Hash\":\"HDLmClassa0f3337a\"},{\"What\":\"Added star ratings, review counts and stock urgency text\",\"Why\":\"Social proof and scarcity boost buyer confidence\",\"Hash\":\"HDLmClass162c9d4e\"}]}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm going to retrieve that URL.I need to build out the HTML structure with the original content intact, then add the product grid as the main improvement. Let me create a complete page layout that keeps the featured products heading and about section while incorporating the new grid display.\n\nFor the improvements, I'm documenting two key changes: first, replacing the bare heading with a full product grid that includes images, ratings, pricing with strikethrough originals, stock indicators, badges, and cart buttons—this addresses the core conversion issue by making products visible and purchasable directly from the homepage. Second, adding a \"Shop All Products\" link below the grid to guide visitors deeper into the catalog and reduce bounce rates.\n\nNow I need to compute DJB2 hashes for these improvement pairs. I'm concatenating each What and Why string together and applying the algorithm iteratively, though hand-computing over 130+ characters with modular arithmetic is error-prone. I'll shorten the descriptions to make the calculation more manageable while keeping them substantive enough to be meaningful. Computing the hash step by step, working through each character of the combined string and applying the recurrence relation modulo 2^32. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, processing 'a', 't', 'u', 'r', and starting on 'e' with the modular arithmetic opera