 and subtractions based on power-of-2 thresholds. Continuing through the character processing... Continuing the hash calculation through the remaining characters of \"Guide's vis\"... Continuing the hash calculation... Continuing the hash computation through the remaining characters of the string, processing each letter and space with the same multiplication and modulo operations. Now I'm converting that hash value to hexadecimal by dividing by successive powers of 16 to extract each digit, which gives me 0xA0F3337A.\n\nI'm thinking about adding a third improvement to the list beyond the two I already have. The third one would cover star ratings, review counts, and stock urgency indicators, though I'd need to compute another hash for it. Two improvements work fine, but three might provide better coverage of the changes. I'm catching a mistake in my hash computation — I skipped the final 'd' in \"Added\" when processing string1, so that hash is incorrect. Let me recalculate string1 properly by including all five characters: A-d-d-e-d, then the space that follows. Now I'm working through the hash computation for string1 starting from where string2 left off. After \"Added a \" the hash is 2059994872, so I'm continuing from there with the remaining characters \"featured product grid with Add to Cart buttonsLets shoppers buy from the homepage, raising conversions\", computing each character's contribution step by step. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: 'd', space, 'p', 'r', and 'o', applying the same pattern of multiplying by 33, adding the character code, and subtracting the appropriate power-of-2 values to keep the result bounded. Continuing through the hash computation for 'grid w'... Continuing the hash computation... Continuing the hash computation for \"button\" by processing each character through the same multiplication and modulo operations. Continuing the hash computation through the remaining characters of \"shops\"... Conti