l signup offer integrated throughout.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The fetch only returned plain text instead of HTML, so I'll need to construct a proper HTML page from that content and incorporate the email signup offer. Let me try a different URL variant to see if I can get the structured HTML directly.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""},{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm adding an email signup offer with a 10% discount incentive positioned above the featured products to capture visitor emails and reduce purchase friction. I need to compute the DJB2 hash for this change.\n\nComputing DJB2 hash... Continuing through the remaining characters, computing each hash step with modular arithmetic to keep the values within 32-bit range. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, tracking how each step multiplies by 33, adds the character value, and compares against powers of 2 to find the difference. Continuing through characters 29-31, computing hash values with modular arithmetic as each character gets processed through the multiplication and addition steps. Continuing the hash computation through characters 35-38, multiplying by 33, adding character values, and taking modulo differences to track the running hash state. Continuing the hash computation through characters 43-46, multiplying by 33, adding character values, and taking modulo operations to keep the values in range. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through space, 'f', 'i', 'r', 's' with their respective modular arithmetic operations to track the running hash value. Continuing the hash computation through characters 'e', space, 'c', 'o', and 'n', applying the same modular arithmetic pattern with successive multiplications by 33, character code additions, and modulo operations to track the running hash value. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters in \"versions,\" tracking how each character's ASCII value combines with the running hash and modulo operations. Continuing through the remaining powers of 16 to extract each hex digit: 7 from 16^5, then 2, 8, 4, 1, and finally 3 as the last digit, giving me 0xC4728413.\n\nI'm verifying this conversion by working backward through the hex value to confirm it reconstructs the original decimal number 3295839251. Now I'm building out the HTML page with navigation, hero section, featured products, the about section, footer, and the email signup offer integrated throughout.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":1,"signature":""}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":6286,"completion_tokens":11840,"total_tokens":18126,"cost":0.32743,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.32743,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.03143,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.296},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":594,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0},"server_tool_use_details":{"web_search_requests":0}}}
