gh the hash computation for the remaining characters: 'a' produces 4038817560, 'n' gives 136993414, 'd' yields 225815466, the space character results in 3156943114, and 'c' starts generating its value from 3156943114 multiplied by 33. Continuing through characters r, s, i, o, and n, applying the same modular arithmetic operations with successive multiplications by 33, additions, and subtractions of powers of 2^32 to keep values in range. 1551686736 - 1543503872 = 8182864, and I'm working through the remaining hex digits by dividing by successive powers of 16, getting 7, C, D, C, 5 with no remainder, so the full hex conversion is 5C7DCC5.\n\nI'm verifying this by adding back all the components: 1543503872 + 7340032 + 786432 + 53248 + 3072 + 80 = 1551686736, which checks out. So Hash1 is HDLmClass5C7CDC50.\n\nNow I'm looking at the second improvement about adding an aggregate rating summary bar with visible ratings and review counts, which is 89 characters, so I need to condense it while keeping the core message intact.\n\nCalculating hash values for \"Added a star rating summary bar\" and \"Visible ratings build instant trust\"... Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, working through the modular arithmetic for each step in the string processing. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: u, m, m, a, and the start of r, applying the same modular arithmetic pattern with successive multiplications by 33 and subtractions of powers of 2^32. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters in \"Visible ratings build instant trust\", processing each letter through the rolling hash algorithm with modular arithmetic to keep values bounded. Continuing the hash computation through the remaining characters, processing 'e', space, 'r', and 'a' with the same multiply-by-33-and-modulo pattern. Continuing the hash computation through the remaining characters, processing the space character and then 'b', 'u', and into 'i', applying the rolling hash formula with modular arithmetic at each step. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: 'n' produces 1826042713, 's' gives 4285627450, 't' yields 3986752494, and now working through 'a' with the same modular arithmetic pattern. Continuing the hash computation through the remaining characters, processing each one through the multiplication and modulo operations to track the rolling hash value as it evolves. Verifying the hex conversion checks out, so the hash is HDLmClass9033D3F3. Now I'm reconstructing the YogaDirect homepage with a header, hero section, featured products, the original about text, a new reviews section, and footer.\n\nBuilding the HTML with inline CSS to include a navigation bar, product grid with yoga mats and accessories, star ratings integrated into each product card, an aggregate rating summary bar as an improvement, customer testimonials with JSON-LD structured data for the reviews section, and all the necessary styling.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm going to retrieve that URL.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""},{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The fetch is returning plain text instead of structured HTML, so I'll need to construct a full HTML page myself that incorporates the content and adds the reviews section that was requested.\n\nFor the improvements, I'm focusing on the reviews scope: adding a customer testimonials section with star ratings and aggregate rating data, plus schema markup to support it. I'll keep the HTML structure simple with the header, featured products, about section, and footer, then layer in the reviews component.\n\nNow I need to compute the hash for the improvements using DJB2 — concatenating the What and Why strings and processing each character through the algorithm. I'll keep the strings concise to make the manual calculation manageable. Computing the hash values... Continuing the hash calcul