 modular arithmetic operations and overflow handling. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, tracking how each step multiplies by 33, adds the character value, and compares against the modulo boundary to calculate the differences. Continuing through the character sequence, processing 'u', 'r', 'i', and 't' with their respective ASCII values and modular arithmetic operations to track the running hash state. Computing hash values for characters 'r', 'o', 'o', and 'f' by applying the rolling hash algorithm with multiplication and modular arithmetic to track the differences. Continuing through the hash calculation for the remaining characters—'b', 'u', 'y'—applying the same multiplication and modulo operations, which gives me a final hash value of 108922250. Verified the hex conversion checks out. Now considering adding a customer review trust badge as a fourth improvement, though that pushes the character count higher. I could keep it to three improvements instead, or add a footer trust seals row with shorter strings to stay within limits. Continuing the hash computation... Continuing the hash computation through characters 17-20, tracking the rolling hash values and their modular arithmetic as I process each character in the string. Continuing through characters 24-27, computing hash values with modular arithmetic and tracking the differences as each character gets processed through the multiplication and addition steps. Continuing the hash calculations through characters 's', 'u', 'r', and 'e', tracking the modular arithmetic and differences at each step. Continuing through characters 41-44, computing hash values with modular arithmetic and tracking differences against powers of 2. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters, tracking how each input character modifies the running hash value through multiplication and addition operations, then comparing against the expected hash boundaries. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters in \"points\", tracking how each character's ASCII value combines with the running hash and comparing against powers of 4294967296. Finishing the hex conversion to verify it matches the hash value, then converting it to the string representation \"5522513b\" before moving on to reconstruct the HTML from the original page.\n\nNow I'm building out the complete page structure with navigation, hero section, trust badges, featured products, about section, and footer — incorporating the new badge elements with their hash classes while keeping the rest of the layout intact.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The fetch only gave me text instead of full HTML, so I'll reconstruct a complete page with the original structure—header, featured products, about section—and add trust badges to build credibility. I'm also computing DJB2 hashes for each improvement to track changes systematically.\n\nComputing DJB2 hashes... Continuing the character-by-character ASCII calculation for the second string, then computing the hash step by step modulo 2^32, starting with h0 = 5381 and processing each character through the multiplication and addition operations. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: space gives 2798155927, then 't' produces 2144832491, 'r' yields 2059995581, and I'm working through 'u' with its multiplication and modulo operations. Continuing through the hash computation for characters 'a', 'n', 'd', and the space that follows, applying the same multiplication and modulo operations to track the rolling hash value as it evolves through each character. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: 'r', 'a', 'n', 't', 'e' with their respective multiplications by 33, modular arithmetic adjustments, and difference calculations to track the running hash value. Continuing through characters 'a', 'd', 'g', and 'e', computing the rolling hash values by multiplyin