al is to add prices and add-to-cart buttons, I'll construct an improved HTML page featuring products with those elements displayed prominently.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""},{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm adding prices with compare-at pricing and savings badges to the featured product cards to make the pricing more transparent and highlight the value customers are getting.\n\nI'm also adding prominent add-to-cart buttons directly on each card so shoppers can purchase without navigating to a separate product page, which should reduce friction and improve conversion rates.\n\nNow I need to compute DJB2 hashes for these improvements by concatenating the \"What\" and \"Why\" descriptions for each one, though the manual calculation is quite tedious with these longer strings. I'm going to stick with shorter, more manageable strings to avoid computation errors. Let me settle on \"Added prices\" combined with \"Prices lift sales\" for the first improvement, and \"Added cart buttons\" with \"Fewer clicks to buy\" for the second. Now I need to manually hash these combined strings to verify the values. Computing the hash value through successive modulo operations, continuing to process each character in the string by multiplying by 33, adding the character code, and reducing modulo 2^32 to keep the value in range. Continuing the hash computation through characters 's', 'P', 'r', and 'i', applying the rolling multiplication and modulo operations at each step to track the running hash value. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: space shifts the value and reduces it modulo the large constant, then l and i follow the same pattern with their respective ASCII values, building up the hash step by step. Continuing through the hash computation for each character in the string, tracking the modular arithmetic and differences at each step. Finishing the hex conversion to get d661d46a, then formatting it as a hash with the HDLmClass prefix. Now I'm working through the character codes for the combined string \"Added cart buttonsFewer clicks to buy\" to continue the calculation. Continuing through steps 10-13 of the hash calculation, working through each character's transformation by multiplying by 33, adding the character code, and applying modular arithmetic to keep the values bounded. Continuing through the hash computation for the remaining characters: s produces a difference of 2500247344, then F adjusts to 903783798, e gives 4055061659, w yields 673048690, and e starts the next iteration at 22210606. Continuing through characters i, c, k, and s with their hash calculations, tracking the running values and differences against the modulo thresholds. Adding more characters to the hash computation, continuing through the remaining letters and tracking the modular arithmetic to extract the hexadecimal representation. The hex conversion checks out. Now I'm building a complete HTML page for YogaDirect with a header, hero section, product cards displaying prices and add-to-cart buttons, an about section, and a sticky cart—I'll mark the modified elements with a class that includes the hash value.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":1,"signature":""}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":6317,"completion_tokens":13723,"total_tokens":20040,"cost":0.37466,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.37466,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.031585,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.343075},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":807,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0},"server_tool_use_details":{"web_search_requests":0}}}
